---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Summary-Statistics Heritability: LD Score Regression (LDSC & Stratified LDSC)";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical proof of the Bulik-Sullivan et al. (2015) fundamental LDSC expectation, intercept diagnostics for confounding, cross-trait genetic correlation (r_g), and functional partitioning with Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC).";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 875 prose words, 13 display and 67 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "11 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 4</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      While individual-level GREML requires massive computational power (<Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(N^2)" /> memory) and access to protected individual genotypes, can we rigorously estimate SNP heritability, detect uncorrected population stratification, and identify cell-type-specific functional enrichments using only publicly accessible GWAS summary statistics? <strong>LD Score Regression (LDSC)</strong> provides the mathematical answer. This deep dive presents the complete mathematical proof of the <strong>Bulik-Sullivan et al. (2015) LDSC theorem</strong>, the regression slope and intercept mechanics, <strong>bivariate cross-trait genetic correlation (<Katex tex="r_g" />)</strong>, and <strong>Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC)</strong> partitioned heritability.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#definition">1. The LD Score Metric</a></li>
        <li><a href="#theorem">2. The Fundamental LDSC Expectation & Mathematical Proof</a></li>
        <li><a href="#confounding">3. Separating Polygenicity from Confounding & The Attenuation Ratio</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bivariate">4. Cross-Trait Genetic Covariance & Correlation (r_g)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sldsc">5. Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC) & Functional Partitioning</a></li>
        <li><a href="#standardized-tau">6. Standardized Effect Size (τ*) & The Baseline-LD Model</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="definition">
      <h2>1. The LD Score Metric</h2>
      <p>
        For any single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) <Katex tex="j" />, its <strong>LD Score (<Katex tex="\ell_j" />)</strong> is defined as the sum of squared Pearson correlation coefficients between SNP <Katex tex="j" /> and all <Katex tex="M" /> common variants across a chromosomal window:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Single-Variant LD Score</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\ell_j = \sum_{k=1}^{M} r_{jk}^2" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="r_{jk}" /> is the pairwise genotype correlation between SNP <Katex tex="j" /> and SNP <Katex tex="k" />, precomputed from external reference panels (e.g. 1000 Genomes European panel or Pan-UKB).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Biologically, a SNP with a high LD score (<Katex tex="\ell_j \approx 100" />) is in strong correlation with many surrounding variants, meaning its marginal GWAS test statistic will capture the tagged variance of many potentially causal variants. Conversely, an isolated SNP (<Katex tex="\ell_j \approx 1" />) tags only itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="theorem">
      <h2>2. The Fundamental LDSC Expectation & Mathematical Proof</h2>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Bulik-Sullivan et al. (2015) Fundamental LDSC Expectation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi_j^2] = 1 + N a + \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\chi_j^2 = z_j^2" /> = GWAS chi-squared association statistic for SNP <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N" /> = Sample size &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="M" /> = Total number of common variants &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="h^2" /> = Narrow-sense SNP heritability &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="1 + Na" /> = Regression intercept (measuring confounding).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> First-Principles Derivation of the LDSC Expectation
        </div>
        <p>
          Let the true polygenic model for standardized phenotype <Katex tex="\mathbf{y}" /> (<Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[y_i] = 0, \operatorname{Var}(y_i) = 1" />) and standardized genotypes <Katex tex="\mathbf{X}" /> (<Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[X_{ij}] = 0, \operatorname{Var}(X_{ij}) = 1" />) be:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}] = \mathbf{0}, \quad \operatorname{Var}(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) = (1 - h^2)\mathbf{I}" display={true} />
          Under the random effects infinitesimal model, true allelic effects <Katex tex="\beta_k" /> are independent random variables with:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\beta_k] = 0, \quad \operatorname{Var}(\beta_k) = \frac{h^2}{M}, \quad \operatorname{Cov}(\beta_j, \beta_k) = 0 \quad (j \ne k)" display={true} />
          The marginal Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) estimator for SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j = \frac{1}{N} \mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{y} = \frac{1}{N} \mathbf{x}_j^T \left(\sum_{k=1}^M \mathbf{x}_k \beta_k + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}\right) = \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k + \frac{1}{N}\mathbf{x}_j^T \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" display={true} />
          The GWAS Z-score is <Katex tex="z_j = \sqrt{N} \hat{\beta}_j = \sqrt{N} \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k + \epsilon'_j" />, where <Katex tex="\epsilon'_j = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\mathbf{x}_j^T \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1 - h^2)" />.
          <br/><br/>
          Squaring the Z-score to obtain the chi-squared statistic <Katex tex="\chi_j^2 = z_j^2" />:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\chi_j^2 = N \left(\sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k\right)^2 + 2\sqrt{N}\left(\sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k\right)\epsilon'_j + (\epsilon'_j)^2" display={true} />
          Taking the expectation conditional on the LD structure:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi_j^2 \mid \mathbf{r}_j] = N \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk}^2 \operatorname{Var}(\beta_k) + \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon'_j) = N \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk}^2 \left(\frac{h^2}{M}\right) + (1 - h^2) \approx \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j + 1" display={true} />
          In the presence of uncorrected population stratification and cryptic relatedness contributing confounding variance <Katex tex="a" />, the sampling variance of <Katex tex="z_j" /> shifts from <Katex tex="1" /> to <Katex tex="1 + Na" />, completing the proof:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi_j^2] = 1 + N a + \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j" display={true} />
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="confounding">
      <h2>3. Separating Polygenicity from Confounding & The Attenuation Ratio</h2>
      <p>
        In massive GWAS cohorts (<Katex tex="N > 100,000" />), the classic genomic control inflation factor <Katex tex="\lambda_{\text{GC}}" /> is invariably elevated (<Katex tex="\lambda_{\text{GC}} > 1.2" />) even in perfectly clean, homogeneous cohorts. This occurs because true polygenicity naturally inflates genome-wide test statistics.
      </p>

      <h3>LDSC Confounding Diagnostics</h3>
      <p>
        LDSC uniquely separates true polygenic signal from population confounding by regressing <Katex tex="\chi_j^2" /> against <Katex tex="\ell_j" />:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Polygenic Heritability (<Katex tex="h^2" />):</strong> Extracted from the <strong>slope</strong> (<Katex tex="\frac{Nh^2}{M}" />). True biological variants exhibit a positive linear relationship with LD score because variants in high-LD regions tag more causal loci.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Confounding (<Katex tex="Na" />):</strong> Extracted from the <strong>intercept</strong> (<Katex tex="1 + Na" />). Population stratification and cryptic relatedness inflate test statistics uniformly across the entire genome, independent of local LD scores.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">LDSC Attenuation Ratio</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{Attenuation Ratio} = \frac{\text{Intercept} - 1}{\operatorname{Mean}(\chi^2) - 1}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          A ratio near 0 (e.g. <Katex tex="< 0.10" />) indicates that <Katex tex="> 90\%" /> of the test statistic inflation is driven by genuine polygenicity rather than confounding.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="bivariate">
      <h2>4. Cross-Trait Genetic Covariance & Correlation (r_g)</h2>
      <p>
        Bivariate LD Score Regression evaluates whether two complex phenotypes share a common genetic architecture without requiring individual-level data or distinct cohorts.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Bivariate Cross-Trait LDSC Expectation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[z_{1j} z_{2j}] = \frac{\rho_0 N_s}{\sqrt{N_1 N_2}} + \frac{\sqrt{N_1 N_2} \rho_g}{M} \ell_j, \qquad r_g = \frac{\rho_g}{\sqrt{h_1^2 h_2^2}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="z_{1j}, z_{2j}" /> = Z-scores for Trait 1 (<Katex tex="N_1" /> samples) and Trait 2 (<Katex tex="N_2" /> samples) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N_s" /> = Sample overlap count &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\rho_0" /> = Phenotypic correlation among overlapping participants &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\rho_g" /> = Genetic covariance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="r_g \in [-1, 1]" /> = Genetic correlation.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The intercept <Katex tex="\frac{\rho_0 N_s}{\sqrt{N_1 N_2}}" /> absorbs all sample overlap bias, ensuring that the genetic correlation estimate <Katex tex="r_g" /> remains completely unbiased even when traits are measured in identical cohorts (e.g. UK Biobank).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="sldsc">
      <h2>5. Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC) & Functional Partitioning</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Stratified LD Score Regression (S-LDSC, Finucane et al. 2015)</strong> extends LDSC to partition SNP heritability across <Katex tex="C" /> overlapping functional genomic categories (e.g. cell-type-specific enhancers, coding exons, conserved regions):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Stratified LDSC Regression Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi_j^2] = 1 + N a + N \sum_{c=1}^{C} \tau_c \ell(j, c), \qquad \ell(j, c) = \sum_{k \in c} r_{jk}^2" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\ell(j, c)" /> = Stratified LD score of SNP <Katex tex="j" /> with respect to annotation category <Katex tex="c" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\tau_c" /> = Raw per-SNP heritability contribution of category <Katex tex="c" /> conditional on all other annotations.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Partitioned Heritability & Fold Enrichment</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Category Heritability & Enrichment Statistic</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="h^2(c) = \sum_{k \in c} \operatorname{Var}(\beta_k) = \sum_{k \in c} \sum_{c'=1}^C \tau_{c'} a_{k c'}, \qquad \operatorname{Enrichment}(c) = \frac{h^2(c) / h^2_{\text{total}}}{|c| / M}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="|c| / M" /> is the proportion of total SNPs belonging to category <Katex tex="c" />. An enrichment <Katex tex="> 1" /> indicates that the category harbors disproportionately more heritability than expected by chance.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="standardized-tau">
      <h2>6. Standardized Effect Size (τ*) & The Baseline-LD Model</h2>
      <p>
        Because functional categories differ dramatically in size (e.g. coding exons span ~1.5% of the genome, whereas repressed heterochromatin spans >50%), comparing raw coefficients <Katex tex="\tau_c" /> across annotations is misleading.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Gazal et al. (2017)</strong> introduced the <strong>standardized effect size (<Katex tex="\tau_c^*" />)</strong>, defined as the proportionate change in per-SNP heritability associated with a one standard deviation increase in the annotation:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Standardized Conditional Effect Size (Gazal et al. 2017)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\tau_c^* = \frac{M \tau_c \operatorname{sd}(c)}{h^2_{\text{total}}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\operatorname{sd}(c) = \sqrt{p_c(1 - p_c)}" /> is the standard deviation of the binary annotation vector. <Katex tex="\tau_c^*" /> enables direct, unbiased comparisons across diverse functional annotations.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Baseline-LD Model</h3>
      <p>
        To prevent false-positive enrichments driven by confounding genomic features, S-LDSC fits a comprehensive <strong>Baseline-LD Model</strong> comprising 75+ functional annotations:
        <br/>
        &bull; Coding, 5' UTR, 3' UTR, promoter, and intronic regions.
        <br/>
        &bull; Epigenetic marks: H3K4me1, H3K4me3, H3K27ac, H3K9ac, and DNase I hypersensitive sites (DHS).
        <br/>
        &bull; Evolutionary conservation: PhastCons and GERP++ scores across mammals.
        <br/>
        &bull; Continuous LD-related annotations: Minor Allele Frequency (MAF) bins, recombination rate, and flanking LD scores.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>LDSC Expectation:</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2] = 1 + Na + \frac{Nh^2}{M}\ell" /> separates true polygenicity (slope) from population stratification confounding (intercept).
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Bivariate LDSC:</strong> Computes genetic correlation <Katex tex="r_g = \rho_g / \sqrt{h_1^2 h_2^2}" /> while completely removing sample overlap bias.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>S-LDSC:</strong> Partitions heritability across functional categories via <Katex tex="\sum \tau_c \ell(j, c)" /> and quantifies enrichment and standardized conditional effects (<Katex tex="\tau^*" />).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: Association Testing & Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 5 to explore single-marker association tests (OLS, Logistic, Score tests), PCA stratification corrections, and the computational evolution of mixed models from GEMMA to BOLT-LMM, fastGWA, and REGENIE.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-association-linear-mixed-models/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 5: Association Testing & LMMs</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
